# =============================================================================
# .harness/.gitignore — keep runtime state out of the repository
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT IS COMMITTED FROM .harness/
#   README.md, scripts/, tasks/, waves/  — the harness itself and the plan.
#
# WHAT IS NEVER COMMITTED
#   Everything below. These are per-machine runtime state: the worktrees
#   themselves, port reservations, lock files, logs, scratch test databases and
#   downloaded binaries.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   Without it, `git add .harness/` commits the worktrees as embedded git
#   repositories. Git records them as a bare gitlink with no contents, so a
#   clone gets empty directories, `git status` reports them as modified
#   constantly, and — worst — an agent's branch name and in-progress state leak
#   into the public history of a repository that is supposed to be about
#   product code.
#
#   It also keeps the downloaded PocketBase binary (~34 MB) and jq out of git,
#   matching upstream's decision not to vendor PocketBase into the repo.

# The isolated working trees. These are real git worktrees of this repository,
# which is exactly why git tries to record them as submodules.
worktrees/

# Port reservations and locks. Machine-local, meaningless to anyone else.
locks/

# Per-agent logs, reports and scratch (throwaway test databases live here).
logs/

# Downloaded tools: PocketBase, jq.
cache/

# Machine-local tuning. The defaults in lib.sh work without this file.
harness.conf
